title: 'karr pick hands the same task to several agents: the pick decision is made
  on a pre-lock snapshot'
updated: 2026-08-10T01:27:58Z
---

Found while verifying the #46 lock fix. `Lock::acquire` is now genuinely
exclusive (t/75-ref-cas-contention.t proves exactly-one-winner across 12
forked contenders), but `karr pick` still hands one task to several agents.

12 parallel `karr pick --move in-progress` on a fresh 12-task board:

    1 x Picked task 1
    4 x Picked task 2
    7 x Picked task 3

Root cause is not the lock. `Cmd/Pick.pm:94` snapshots `@tasks` via
`load_tasks` and filters out claimed ones at line 107 -- all of that happens
*before* any lock is taken. After `acquire` succeeds at line 146 the task is
never re-read, so its `claimed_by` is never re-checked under the lock. The
lock is then released at line 181, and the next agent -- still holding a
snapshot in which the task looked unclaimed -- acquires the now-free lock and
claims the same task again.

So the lock serialises the agents but does not stop any of them from acting
on a stale decision. Fix is to re-read the task ref after acquiring its lock
and skip it if it has been claimed (or its status changed) in the meantime,
i.e. move the "is this pickable" check inside the locked region.

Out of scope for #46, which was about acquire being atomic and write_ref not
leaking raw libgit2 errors -- both of which are done.
Fixed and merged into main; full suite green at 88 files / 566 tests.

Every one of these was reproduced before it was fixed and left a regression test
that was verified to fail against the pre-fix code -- not a test that merely
passes now. Details and what was deliberately NOT fixed are in the merge commit
that names this ticket.
